import{_ as o,o as n,c as t,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.BUOFcz_J.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"OpenCode through the standard boundaries","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"records/opencode-episode.md","filePath":"records/opencode-episode.md"}'),a={name:"records/opencode-episode.md"};function i(d,e,r,h,c,l){return n(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Historical record</p><p>This page records what happened on a date, with its observed failures and limits. It is not a requirement for the current implementation. Current behaviour is in <a href="/Rho/docs/reference/status">Status</a>.</p></div><h1 id="opencode-through-the-standard-boundaries" tabindex="-1">OpenCode through the standard boundaries <a class="header-anchor" href="#opencode-through-the-standard-boundaries" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;OpenCode through the standard boundaries&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Date: 2026-11-07. Source changes after Rho 0.5.6; Core and Plugins unchanged.</p><h2 id="question" tabindex="-1">Question <a class="header-anchor" href="#question" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Question&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The test from the <a href="/Rho/docs/records/session-continuity-episode">session continuity</a> review: can an Agent that Rho has never been checked with join through standard boundaries alone? Three paths were run separately: the Agent uses Rho from its own window (MCP), the person talks to it in Rho (ACP), and one session moves between the two. OpenCode 1.19.5 was installed and configured on this Mac with its own model (GLM-5.3-Flash through the person&#39;s gateway). Nothing in Rho named OpenCode.</p><p>Rho ran from source on a disposable root and state with <code>--agent-backend acp --acp-command &#39;[&quot;opencode&quot;,&quot;acp&quot;]&#39;</code>. The project was disposable. OpenCode used its own configuration with no auto-approval. The person&#39;s global OpenCode configuration was checked by checksum before and after, and was unchanged. Scripts and raw streams are under <code>work/opencode/</code>.</p><h2 id="what-opencode-declares-over-acp" tabindex="-1">What OpenCode declares over ACP <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-opencode-declares-over-acp" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What OpenCode declares over ACP&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The handshake, with no prompt, returned <code>agentInfo</code> <code>OpenCode 1.19.5</code> and these capabilities:</p><ul><li><code>loadSession</code>, plus session <code>list</code>, <code>resume</code>, <code>fork</code> and <code>close</code>.</li><li>MCP over HTTP and SSE, in addition to ACP&#39;s baseline stdio.</li><li>Three settings: model (104 choices), effort and mode.</li><li><code>session/list</code> filtered by folder, returning each session&#39;s title and update time.</li></ul><p>Rho&#39;s generic ACP driver needs nothing beyond these.</p><h2 id="using-rho-from-opencode-s-own-window" tabindex="-1">Using Rho from OpenCode&#39;s own window <a class="header-anchor" href="#using-rho-from-opencode-s-own-window" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Using Rho from OpenCode&#39;s own window&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Rho&#39;s setup prompt was given to <code>opencode run</code> unchanged. OpenCode loaded its own configuration skill and ran the grant command without printing the reply. It wrote the MCP server into a project-scoped <code>.opencode/opencode.json</code> (mode 600) and left the global file alone. It then checked <code>rho_index</code> directly and said that a new session was needed to load the tools. That session called <code>rho_index</code> (the project ID matched) and wrote <code>notes/plan.md</code>. Rho listed the connection as <code>opencode-glm</code> with a verified call.</p><p>Rho&#39;s Conversations listed none of OpenCode&#39;s sessions. Rho has no reader for them.</p><h2 id="talking-to-opencode-in-rho" tabindex="-1">Talking to OpenCode in Rho <a class="header-anchor" href="#talking-to-opencode-in-rho" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Talking to OpenCode in Rho&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A conversation started in Rho ran under OpenCode&#39;s own session ID. In the same conversation it:</p><ul><li>read a file;</li><li>called <code>rho_index</code> through the MCP server Rho passes at session start;</li><li>edited a file, and Rho recorded the change;</li><li>reported its usage and cost.</li></ul><p>The message box offered OpenCode&#39;s models and effort.</p><h2 id="one-session-in-both-places" tabindex="-1">One session in both places <a class="header-anchor" href="#one-session-in-both-places" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;One session in both places&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Rho to terminal.</strong> <code>opencode run --session</code> quoted, word for word, the line Rho had asked OpenCode to add.</li><li><strong>Terminal back to Rho.</strong> Rho&#39;s connection to OpenCode was still open. Its next message got the code word given in the terminal. OpenCode reads history from its database each turn, so the open connection was not stale.</li><li><strong>Terminal session continued over ACP.</strong> <code>session/list</code> returned the three terminal sessions in this folder in 440 ms. The generic driver continued <code>Rho first task</code> and answered with its code word. Rho&#39;s UI cannot do this yet, because it does not list these sessions.</li><li><strong>Open in OpenCode&#39;s interactive window.</strong> While the window held the session open, Rho sent into it and was not refused. The window&#39;s next prompt then quoted Rho&#39;s message. OpenCode tolerates alternating writers. Rho&#39;s refusal while a session is open elsewhere was written for Claude Code and is that Agent&#39;s behaviour, not a general rule.</li></ul><h2 id="observation-through-opencode-s-own-plugin" tabindex="-1">Observation through OpenCode&#39;s own plugin <a class="header-anchor" href="#observation-through-opencode-s-own-plugin" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Observation through OpenCode&#39;s own plugin&quot;">​</a></h2><p>As an experiment, the project got a 20-line plugin in <code>.opencode/plugins/</code>. It appends OpenCode&#39;s plugin events to <code>.rho/agent/inbox/opencode.jsonl</code>: a local file, with no network and no Rho code.</p><p>It recorded, as they happened:</p><ul><li>session creation, title and busy/idle status;</li><li>each message and its cost;</li><li>each tool call, with its command, output and exit code.</li></ul><p>Records came from the interactive window, from <code>opencode run</code> and from Rho&#39;s own <code>opencode acp</code> process, each with its process ID.</p><p>It also carried the model&#39;s reasoning text, which Rho must not keep, and OpenCode&#39;s startup noise. With the filter used, the person&#39;s prompt text did not come through. On the first run after the plugin was added, OpenCode stopped after <code>init</code> for over five minutes and was stopped by hand. Three later runs started normally, and the cause was not found.</p><h2 id="found-and-fixed" tabindex="-1">Found and fixed <a class="header-anchor" href="#found-and-fixed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Found and fixed&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Rho named OpenCode &quot;ACP Agent&quot; and drew the DeepSeek mark on its conversations. The application assumed that any ACP Agent was Harness. Rho already recorded the name the Agent reports in its ACP handshake. Now:</p><ul><li>A configured ACP Agent is named as it named itself, or by its command until it has answered. The existing Harness name is kept, because Harness reports <code>deepseek-harness-acp</code>.</li><li>The Agents tab, conversation rows, the panel title, the message box and the settings menu draw each Agent&#39;s mark by its name. An Agent without a mark shows its initial.</li></ul><p>After the change, OpenCode appears as &quot;OpenCode&quot;, with an &quot;O&quot;, in each of these places. 54 related tests and the Agent page acceptance in Chrome pass. The Harness case was not run again after this change.</p><h2 id="not-fixed" tabindex="-1">Not fixed <a class="header-anchor" href="#not-fixed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Not fixed&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Listing.</strong> OpenCode sessions started outside Rho are not listed, so they cannot be continued from Rho. Listing over <code>session/list</code> starts the Agent&#39;s process for a read. The inbox plugin needs no process, but only covers sessions after the plugin was installed.</li><li><strong>Turns from other windows.</strong> Turns sent to Rho&#39;s own OpenCode session from the terminal or the interactive window do not appear in Rho&#39;s panel.</li><li><strong>&quot;Open elsewhere&quot; is fixed per Agent.</strong> It is coded for Claude Code and Codex. It should come from what each Agent does.</li><li><strong>One ACP command per service.</strong> A conversation follows whichever ACP command the service has now, not the one that started it. (Fixed later the same day: <a href="/Rho/docs/records/agents-in-rho-episode">Adding Agents that Rho starts</a>.)</li><li><strong>Brand code remains in the application.</strong> Continuation routing and the Harness checks still branch on Claude Code, Codex and Harness.</li></ul><h2 id="limits" tabindex="-1">Limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Limits&quot;">​</a></h2><p>One model, one machine (macOS) and one person&#39;s configuration. The plugin was an experiment in a disposable project and is not shipped. The interactive window was driven through a pseudo-terminal, and its screen was not read. No npm release was made.</p>',33)])])}const g=o(a,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
